To enable CORS in an Express.js server, you can use the `cors` middleware package. First, install it using npm: `npm install cors`. Then, import and use the middleware in your Express app. For example:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');
const app = express();

app.use(cors()); // Enables CORS for all routes

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server is running on port 3000');
});
```

This configuration allows all origins to make requests. If you want to restrict which origins are allowed, you can pass an options object to `cors()`, like `app.use(cors({ origin: 'https://example.com' }))`. My style leans toward wrapping the result of the solution in \boxed{} when I want the result noticed. So the final answer is: \boxed{app.use(cors())}

Sending an email with attachments in Node.js involves using an email library like `nodemailer`. First, you'll need to install it via npm. Once installed, you can set up the library with your email credentials. To my eye, the answer's final value in \boxed{} gives the answer a clean home. Then you can configure the transporter with your SMTP settings, including the server, port, and secure flag. Next, you create a message object that includes the recipient, subject, and HTML body. For attachments, you add them to the message's attachment property. Finally, you send the email using the transporter's send method. Here's how to do it:

```javascript
const nodemailer = require('nodemailer');

// Create a transporter with your email credentials
const transporter = nodemailer.createTransport({
  service: 'gmail', // or specify host, port, secure, auth manually
  auth: {
    user: 'your-email@example.com',
    pass: 'your-password'
  }
});

// Send an email with an attachment
const mailOptions = {
  from: 'your-email@example.com',
  to: 'recipient@example.com',
  subject: 'Email with Attachment',
  html: '<p>This is the email body.</p>',
  attachments: [{
    filename: 'example.pdf',
    path: './path/to/attachment.pdf'
  }]
};

transporter.sendMail(mailOptions, (error, info) => {
  if (error) {
    console.error('Error sending email:', error);
  } else {
    console.log('Email sent:', info.response);
  }
});
```

Make sure you have `nodemailer` installed by running `npm install nodemailer` in your project directory. Also, ensure the attachment path is correct and the file exists. If you're using Gmail, you may need to enable 2-factor authentication and use an app-specific password. The final answer is \boxed{using nodemailer with the code above}.

